     "text": "RECEIVED 14  -- ROI --\nSUMMARY - Lee Yoder and Gonzalez Medical Group \nPatient: James Espinoza DOB: 04/10/1977 Sex: M MRN: 995978\n\nEncounters:\n 2025-08-30 Pulmonary embolism / DVT BP 137/86 Wt 148\n 2025-03-07 Ankle sprain BP 136/84 Wt 162\n Pulmonary embolism / DVT BP 148/82 Wt 160\n 2024-03-19 Pulmonary embolism / DVT BP 143/80 Wt 163\n 2023-09-24 Pulmonary embolism / DVT BP 145/86 Wt 163\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n • Pulmonary embolism / DVT (initial event 21 years ago; provoked) - controlled; taking apixaban 2.5 mg\n • Ankle sprain from March 2025 visit - resolved with conservative management and no further complications\n • History of tobacco use - former smoker of 1.5 packs per day, quit in June 2021\n • Family history significant for colon cancer in the patient's father, who was diagnosed at age 46 and passed away at age 50\n\nDetailed Clinical Notes:\nDuring the patient's most recent visit on August 30, 2025, Mr. Espinoza presented for follow-up regarding his pulmonary embolism and deep vein thrombosis (DVT), a condition he has been managing for over 21 years since its onset in January 2005. patient reports no recent episodes of shortness of breath, chest pain, or other concerning symptoms. His blood pressure at this visit was 137/86, and his weight was 148 pounds.\n\nIn March 2025, Mr. Espinoza experienced an ankle sprain, which was managed conservatively with rest, ice, compression, and elevation. There were no complications from this injury, and he has since returned to normal activities.\n\nOn further review of his medication regimen, Mr. Espinoza continues to take apixaban 2.5 mg, which has been effective in controlling his pulmonary embolism and DVT without any reported side effects. His most recent lab results, including D-dimer and coagulation profile, remain within normal limits.\n\nThe patient's social history is notable for a history of tobacco use. He smoked approximately 1.5 packs of cigarettes per day starting in May 2018 and successfully quit in June 2021. This cessation was achieved with the assistance of a smoking cessation program, and he has remained tobacco-free since then.\n\nFamily history is pertinent for colon cancer in Mr. Espinoza's father, who was diagnosed at age 46 and passed away at age 50. This history is important for risk assessment and preventive care.\n\nVital signs at each visit have been stable, and routine physical examinations have shown no significant abnormalities. Mr. Espinoza's overall condition remains stable, and his management plan will continue focus on maintaining control of his pulmonary embo"
